# Supabase Configuration
# Get these from: Supabase Dashboard > Settings > API
#
# Both are REQUIRED in every environment, including for `npm run build` — they
# are validated at startup by src/lib/env-public.ts, so a missing value fails
# with one readable message instead of a confusing runtime error.

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...

# Optional: Service role key for server-side operations (keep secret!)
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...

# Email Configuration (Resend)
# Get your API key from: https://resend.com/api-keys
# Leave unset locally: emails are logged to the console instead of sent.
# (Uncomment only when you actually want to send — a non-empty placeholder makes
# the app try the network and fail.)
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_your_api_key_here

# Optional: Custom sender email address
# Format: `Name <local@domain>` or a bare `local@domain`.
# Default outside production: onboarding@resend.dev (Resend's test sender, which
# delivers ONLY to the Resend account owner's mailbox).
# EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=Holigay Vendor Market <noreply@yourdomain.com>

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Production strictness
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vercel sets VERCEL_ENV itself; never set it in .env.local.
#
# When VERCEL_ENV=production (a Production deploy of `main`), RESEND_API_KEY and
# EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS become REQUIRED and a sender on the resend.dev domain is
# refused — the build fails rather than silently emailing from an address only
# the Resend account owner can receive. Preview deploys (VERCEL_ENV=preview) and
# local builds stay lenient. NODE_ENV is deliberately NOT used for this decision:
# Vercel builds previews with NODE_ENV=production.
#
# Full contract: specs/007-production-readiness/contracts/env-contract.md

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keep-alive (Vercel Production only — never in .env.local)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both hosted Supabase projects are on the free tier, which pauses a project
# after about seven days without API activity. A daily Vercel cron calls
# /api/keepalive, which reads one row from every project listed below.
#
# Neither variable is a build-time requirement: unset, the app deploys fine and
# the route answers 401 (no secret) or 500 `misconfigured` (no targets).
#
# CRON_SECRET: at least 16 characters (generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`); a
# shorter value is treated as unset. Vercel sends it as
# `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>` on cron invocations. It is deliberately
# not shown as KEY=value here — a copied placeholder is long enough to be
# accepted as a real secret.
#
# KEEPALIVE_SUPABASE_TARGETS: `url|anonKey` pairs, comma-separated. List BOTH
# projects explicitly — production's own project is not inferred from the
# deployment, so a half-filled list would silently protect only one.
# KEEPALIVE_SUPABASE_TARGETS=https://dev-ref.supabase.co|eyJ...,https://prod-ref.supabase.co|eyJ...
#
# To try the route locally, put both in the shell (not this file, not
# .env.local) — see specs/007-production-readiness/quickstart.md.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Smoke check (shell only — never set these in .env.local)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `npm run smoke` (scripts/smoke-check.mjs) reads these three from the shell and exits
# 2 if any is missing. They are listed here for discoverability only: the script loads
# no env file at all, deliberately, so that a production host cannot end up committed
# by way of a checked-in .env.
#
# SMOKE_APP_URL: the origin to probe, no trailing slash — a production or preview
#   deployment, or http://localhost:3000 with `npm run dev` running.
# SMOKE_SUPABASE_URL: that environment's project API URL — the same value as its
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL.
# SMOKE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY: the anon key, NEVER the service role key. The five checks
#   prove what an anonymous visitor can and cannot reach; a service role key would
#   pass all of them while proving nothing.
#
# SMOKE_APP_URL=https://<host> SMOKE_SUPABASE_URL=https://<ref>.supabase.co SMOKE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<anon key> npm run smoke
#
# Full procedure, including the event-week click-through: docs/runbooks/event-week-smoke.md
